  [38;5;153mspec[39m maps a field name to an array of rules. Rules run in order and the [1mfirst failing rule per field wins[0m — one
  message per field, which is what a form actually displays. Fields not in the spec are ignored; a spec field absent
  from [38;5;153mvalues[39m arrives as [38;5;153mundefined[39m, so [38;5;153mrequired()[39m catches it.

  [1mBuilt-ins:[0m [38;5;153mrequired[39m, [38;5;153mminLength[39m, [38;5;153mmaxLength[39m, [38;5;153mpattern[39m, [38;5;153memail[39m, [38;5;153mmatches[39m (cross-field). Each takes an optional custom
  message as its last argument. A project-specific rule is a plain function — nothing to register.

  [34mconst[39m loginSpec = {
    [36musername[39m: [[33mrequired[39m([31m"Username is required"[39m)],
    [36mpassword[39m: [[33mrequired[39m(), [33mminLength[39m([32m8[39m)],
  };

  [1mOne consequence to flag now:[0m [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m is blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m by CORS, so [38;5;153mindex.html[39m will stop working
  by double-clicking it. You'll need a static server — [38;5;153mpython3 -m http.server[39m in the repo root. That's inherent to the
  ESM choice, not to this design. Tell me if double-click-to-open matters and I'll revisit the module decision rather
  than work around it.

  Alongside the architecture, the tooling question — this repo has none configured, and it's cheapest to set up before
  the code exists:
